          "question": "I have been getting heartburn most evenings.",
          "answer": "**Urgency: routine, based on your description.** The symptom check found no emergency pattern in the red-flag table for \"heartburn most evenings.\" That is not a clearance. The table covers a fixed list of presentations, so please seek medical advice if the pattern changes.\n\n**Call emergency services now if you have any of these:**\n- Chest pain, pressure, or tightness, especially with pain spreading to the arm, jaw, or back, or with breathlessness, sweating, or nausea\n- Shortness of breath\n- Vomiting blood, or vomit that looks like coffee grounds\n- Black, tarry stools\n- Pain or difficulty when swallowing, if it is new or getting worse\n\nHeartburn and heart-related chest pain can feel alike. A clinical guideline in the retrieved material says a history of reflux does not rule out a heart cause, and that antacid relief does not rule it out either. If your evening discomfort ever feels different, comes with breathlessness, or spreads to the arm or jaw, treat it as an emergency.\n\n**Body systems involved:** I could not finish the body-system grouping because the tool budget ran out for this conversation. From the material I did retrieve, the pattern you describe mainly concerns the digestive system (the food pipe and stomach). The heart and chest are also worth ruling out, as explained above.\n\n**Next step:** Because this happens most evenings, book a routine appointment with a doctor. They can ask about meal timing, weight, smoking, alcohol, and medicines that can worsen reflux, and can decide whether any treatment is appropriate for you. Some guidance suggests that for typical symptoms with no warning features, a doctor may first try a time-limited course of acid-reducing treatment before arranging tests. Do not start or change any medicine without speaking to a clinician or pharmacist first.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
